- POST /api/evidence/{evidenceKey}/weight-review with actor header.
- GET /api/evidence/batches/{intakeKey}.

Canonical hash for batch intake: build sorted pair list. Add private method in controller:

```java
private String batchHash(BatchIntakeRequest request) {
    List<Map<String, Object>> pairs = new ArrayList<>();
    List<BatchIntakeItem> items = request.items() == null ? List.of() : request.items();
    List<BigDecimal> weights = request.measuredWeights() == null ? List.of() : request.measuredWeights();
    for (int i = 0; i < items.size(); i++) {
        Map<String, Object> pair = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        BatchIntakeItem item = items.get(i);
        pair.put("evidenceKey", item == null ? null : item.evidenceKey());
        pair.put("description", item == null ? null : item.description());
        pair.put("declaredWeight", item == null || item.declaredWeight() == null ? null : item.declaredWeight().stripTrailingZeros());
        pair.put("measuredWeight", i < weights.size() && weights.get(i) != null ? weights.get(i).stripTrailingZeros() : null);
        pairs.add(pair);
    }
    pairs.sort(Comparator.comparing(p -> String.valueOf(p.get("evidenceKey"))));
    return idempotencyAdvisor.hash(EvidenceService.OP_BATCH_INTAKE, request.custodianId(), null, pairs);
}
```

Wait — stripTrailingZeros on BigDecimal like 0.00 → 0E-10? stripTrailingZeros of "0.00" gives 0E+2... Actually new BigDecimal("0.00").stripTrailingZeros() = 0E+2, serialized as "0E+2" by Jackson? Jackson writes BigDecimal via toString → "0E+2". That's fine for hashing consistency (both 0.00 and 0.0 → 0E+2? "0.0".stripTrailingZeros() = 0E+1. Hmm inconsistent but weights must be positive anyway). Fine.

Note: hash includes custodianId as actorId param. But bean validation: intakeKey/custodianId @NotBlank — validation happens before controller body executes (@Valid). If custodianId null → 400 before hash. Good. But items could contain nulls — handled.

